\title{Computing Road-Network Connectivity and Modeled Connections\newline among Paris Subcentres}
\author{}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Study objects and coordinate system}

The analysis uses the 44 existing classified subcentre polygons in the Paris
study area, their saved functional types, and the project's urban-centre point
(CBD). Subcentre selection and functional classification are retained without
re-estimation. The map label RCOI denotes the existing all-high functional
combination previously labelled Mixed: residential, commercial, office, and
industrial. The residential variable is an OpenStreetMap address proxy.

The road input contains 661,838 features within the circular Paris study area
of radius 99.56~km. It includes motorways, trunk, primary, secondary, tertiary,
unclassified, residential, living-street and service roads, and the available
motorway, trunk, primary, secondary and tertiary links. Railway lines and
additional walking-path categories are not included.

Road geometries, subcentre polygons and the CBD are transformed to
RGF93 / Lambert-93 (EPSG:2154). Computations use projected metre coordinates;
reported route distances are converted to kilometres. Map display uses
EPSG:3035, which does not change the previously calculated network distances.

\section{Construction of the road graph}

Let $G=(V,E)$ be the undirected road graph. Road geometries are separated into
individual line strings, and consecutive existing vertices within each line
string are connected by edges. Coordinates are quantized to a 0.01~m grid
to identify shared vertices. The coordinate of the first occurrence is retained
as the representative node location.

For an original consecutive coordinate pair
$\mathbf{q}_i=(x_i,y_i)$ and $\mathbf{q}_{i+1}=(x_{i+1},y_{i+1})$, the edge
weight is its Euclidean segment length in metres:
\begin{equation}
  \ell_e=\left\|\mathbf{q}_{i+1}-\mathbf{q}_i\right\|_2
  =\sqrt{(x_{i+1}-x_i)^2+(y_{i+1}-y_i)^2}.
  \label{eq:edge-length}
\end{equation}
Consecutive coordinates assigned to the same node are discarded. Self-loops
are removed, and parallel edges connecting the same node pair are collapsed
to the minimum available segment length. Thus, edge weights retain the
original segment lengths rather than being recalculated from rounded
coordinates.

Connections are introduced only at shared existing geometry vertices.
Geometrically crossing lines are not automatically split at their crossings.
The resulting graph contains 3,667,993 vertices and 3,881,228 edges. Node degree
is calculated on this complete graph before assigning nodes to subcentre
polygons:
\begin{equation}
  k_v=\left|\{u\in V:\{u,v\}\in E\}\right|.
  \label{eq:degree}
\end{equation}

\section{Local connectivity within each subcentre}

For subcentre polygon $P_s$, let $A_s$ be its projected polygon area expressed
in square kilometres. Define the nodes intersecting that polygon as
\begin{equation}
  V_s=\{v\in V:\mathbf{q}_v\in P_s,\ k_v>0\}.
\end{equation}
Boundary intersections are included. The implementation checks that no
assigned node intersects more than one subcentre polygon. Degrees are inherited
from the complete road graph, so subcentre boundaries do not create artificial
dead ends by clipping roads.

Degree-two vertices are retained for routing but excluded from street-node
and junction measures. This prevents ordinary geometry points along curved
roads from being counted as intersections. Define
\begin{align}
  T_s &= \{v\in V_s:k_v\ne 2\}, &
  J_s &= \{v\in V_s:k_v\ge 3\}, &
  D_s &= \{v\in V_s:k_v=1\}.
\end{align}
Here, $T_s$ contains street nodes after excluding degree-two vertices,
$J_s$ contains junctions, and $D_s$ contains dead ends.

Street-node density and junction density are
\begin{equation}
  \rho_s^{T}=\frac{|T_s|}{A_s},
  \qquad
  \rho_s^{J}=\frac{|J_s|}{A_s}.
  \label{eq:densities}
\end{equation}
Junction density, in junctions per square kilometre, is the primary local
connectivity indicator. Mean street-node degree, mean junction degree, and
the dead-end fraction are
\begin{align}
  \overline{k}_s^{T}
    &=\frac{1}{|T_s|}\sum_{v\in T_s}k_v, &
  \overline{k}_s^{J}
    &=\frac{1}{|J_s|}\sum_{v\in J_s}k_v, &
  f_s^{D}&=\frac{|D_s|}{|T_s|}.
  \label{eq:local-degree}
\end{align}
Measures with zero denominators are recorded as missing. The outputs also
report the counts of all graph vertices, degree-two vertices, dead ends,
and junctions with degree at least four.

Let $V^{\mathrm{LCC}}$ denote the largest connected component of the complete
graph. The fraction of a subcentre's assigned graph vertices belonging to it is
\begin{equation}
  g_s=\frac{|V_s\cap V^{\mathrm{LCC}}|}{|V_s|}.
\end{equation}
The number of complete-graph components represented inside each polygon is
also recorded. These component measures are not calculated from a separately
clipped subcentre graph.

\section{Centroids and road-node representatives}

Each subcentre is represented initially by its true area-weighted geometric
centroid, calculated from the complete projected polygon, including multipart
geometry and holes:
\begin{equation}
  \mathbf{c}_s=
  \frac{1}{\mu(P_s)}\int_{P_s}\mathbf{q}\,\mathrm{d}\mu(\mathbf{q}),
  \label{eq:centroid}
\end{equation}
where $\mu$ denotes area in square metres. An interior label point is not
substituted for the centroid; a centroid may lie outside a concave polygon.
The CBD, denoted $s=0$, uses the existing project centre point rather than the
centroid of the urban boundary.

For routing, the centroid is snapped to the nearest nonisolated road vertex
in the complete graph:
\begin{equation}
  r_s=\underset{v\in V:\,k_v>0}{\arg\min}
      \left\|\mathbf{c}_s-\mathbf{q}_v\right\|_2,
  \qquad
  a_s=\left\|\mathbf{c}_s-\mathbf{q}_{r_s}\right\|_2.
  \label{eq:snapping}
\end{equation}
The offset $a_s$ is reported in metres. The selected vertex is not required
to lie inside the subcentre polygon or in the largest graph component.

\section{Distances between subcentres and to the CBD}

Let $\mathcal{P}(r_s,r_t)$ be the set of paths joining the snapped vertices
of locations $s$ and $t$. Dijkstra's algorithm gives the length-weighted
shortest-path distance:
\begin{equation}
  d_G(s,t)=\frac{1}{1000}
       \min_{\pi\in\mathcal{P}(r_s,r_t)}\sum_{e\in\pi}\ell_e.
  \label{eq:network-distance}
\end{equation}
Distances are in kilometres. Applying Eq.~\eqref{eq:network-distance} to
$t=0$ gives each subcentre's network distance to the CBD. The corresponding
centroid-to-centroid Euclidean reference distance is
\begin{equation}
  d_E(s,t)=\frac{\|\mathbf{c}_s-\mathbf{c}_t\|_2}{1000}.
\end{equation}

Network distances exclude off-network snapping offsets. A separate descriptive
quantity adds the two straight-line offsets:
\begin{equation}
  d_G^{+}(s,t)=d_G(s,t)+\frac{a_s+a_t}{1000}.
\end{equation}
These added connectors are not surveyed or validated road segments and are
not used to trace the mapped routes.

The 44 subcentres produce
\begin{equation}
  N_{\mathrm{pairs}}=\binom{44}{2}=946
\end{equation}
unique unordered pairs. All pairs, together with the 44 CBD connections, are
stored in a symmetric $45\times45$ distance matrix with zero diagonal. If
locations are disconnected, the network distance is recorded as missing and
an explicit connectivity flag is exported.

For each subcentre, reachable-subcentre count and mean distance to reachable
subcentres are calculated as
\begin{align}
  \mathcal{R}_s&=\{t\ne s:d_G(s,t)<\infty\},\\
  n_s^{\mathrm{reach}}&=|\mathcal{R}_s|,
  \qquad
  \overline{d}_{G,s}
     =\frac{1}{|\mathcal{R}_s|}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{R}_s}d_G(s,t).
\end{align}
The mean is missing when no other subcentre is reachable. In the constructed
Paris graph, all 946 pairs and all 44 CBD connections are connected.

\section{Modeled connections and shared-route intensity}

One shortest path $\pi_{st}^{\star}$ is retained for every unordered
subcentre pair $1\le s<t\le44$. Every pair receives an equal unit weight;
no population, POI, employment, or observed trip-volume weighting is applied.
If equal-length alternatives exist, the algorithm selects one; demand is not
split between them.

The number of selected pair routes using road edge $e$ is
\begin{equation}
  F_e=\sum_{s=1}^{43}\sum_{t=s+1}^{44}
      \mathbb{1}\{e\in\pi_{st}^{\star}\},
  \label{eq:route-count}
\end{equation}
and its share of the 946 pair routes is
\begin{equation}
  p_e=100\,\frac{F_e}{946}.
\end{equation}
The complete paths are exported as polylines. Road segments with $F_e>0$
are mapped using increasing red intensity and line width, with a square-root
display transformation to distinguish lower counts. Functional subcentre
polygons, labels, the CBD, available building footprints, and the INSEE
urban-area boundary provide spatial context. The CBD is displayed for
orientation, but CBD routes are excluded from Eq.~\eqref{eq:route-count}.

These counts describe modeled route overlap among the selected subcentres.
They are not observed traffic volumes, measured commuting flows, or
all-network edge betweenness. The mapped routes use 183,189 graph edges;
the maximum is 106 pair routes on one edge.

\section{Internal checks and interpretation}

The implementation checks that the distance matrix is symmetric with zero
diagonal and that all 946 unordered pairs are present once. Each traced path
length is compared with its previously calculated pair distance, using a
tolerance of $10^{-6}$~km. Edge-use totals satisfy the consistency identity
\begin{equation}
  \sum_{e\in E}F_e
    =\sum_{s=1}^{43}\sum_{t=s+1}^{44}|\pi_{st}^{\star}|,
\end{equation}
where $|\pi_{st}^{\star}|$ is the number of graph edges on the selected path.
These checks establish consistency between the computed tables, traced paths,
and map layers; they do not establish the validity of individual travel routes.

The graph is undirected and does not incorporate one-way restrictions,
turn restrictions, access rules, barriers, travel speeds, or travel times.
Original OSM node identifiers are unavailable in the geometry input, so
grade separation cannot be guaranteed: coincident existing geometry vertices
can be conflated, although new intersections are not inserted at line
crossings. Nearby junctions, roundabouts, and dual carriageways are not
consolidated. Road coverage is restricted to the study extent, so paths
leaving that extent are unavailable. Results should therefore be interpreted
as connectivity of the available geometry-based road graph rather than
validated driving or pedestrian accessibility.

\section{Reproducible implementation}

The computations are implemented in the following R files in the same folder
as this document:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \nolinkurl{214_compute_paris_subcentre_network_connectivity.R}:
    graph construction, local node metrics, centroid snapping, pair distances,
    and CBD distances.
  \item \nolinkurl{215_map_all_paris_subcentre_network_connections.R}:
    tracing the 946 shortest paths, counting shared-road use, and producing
    the connection map with RCOI in the legend.
\end{itemize}
The implementation uses \texttt{sf}, \texttt{igraph}, \texttt{FNN},
\texttt{dplyr}, \texttt{readr}, \texttt{ggplot2}, \texttt{ggrepel},
\texttt{terra}, and \texttt{ragg}. Outputs include subcentre-level connectivity
tables, pairwise distance tables, centroid snapping records, the distance
matrix, route-count tables, and GeoPackage layers.

